<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">First flight</h2>
  <h3>Flash it, configure it, fly it.</h3>
  <p class="lede">
    A skyBlip Go leaves nothing to install beyond the firmware itself: drag a
    file onto the device, then talk to it over Bluetooth. This page is the
    whole of that&nbsp;&mdash; the first flash, the settings a phone can
    change, what the three alarm levels actually do &mdash; and it ends with
    the part a stranger flying this for the first time needs most: what the
    device does not see.
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">The first flash</h2>
  <h3>Double-click, drag, drop.</h3>
  <p>
    The LilyGO T-Echo Plus that skyBlip Go runs on ships with a factory
    bootloader already installed, the same one LilyGO&rsquo;s own firmware and
    projects like SoftRF and Meshtastic use. It never gets touched, in either
    direction: installing skyBlip does not remove your ability to put a
    different firmware back on hardware you own.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      Connect the device by USB-C, then double-click its RESET
      button within half a second.
    </li>
    <li>
      A drive named <span class="mono">TECHOBOOT</span> appears on your
      computer. This is the bootloader itself talking, independent of
      whatever firmware happens to be on the device &mdash; including a
      broken or half-written one.
    </li>
    <li>
      Drag the <span class="mono">.uf2</span> file onto that drive, and wait
      for the copy to finish. The device reboots on its own, running the new
      firmware.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Every <span class="mono">.uf2</span> we publish is built to fit entirely
    inside the region the bootloader reserves for an application, so a
    correctly built one cannot land on the bootloader itself. The one file to
    stay away from is a <em>bootloader replacement</em> for this board,
    sometimes shared alongside other T-Echo firmware under a name like
    <span class="mono">nosd.uf2</span>: dropping one removes the drag-and-drop
    path that this page depends on, with no way back except a hardware
    programmer. Nothing skyBlip publishes is that file.
  </p>
  <p class="note">
    The same double-click always works, whatever state the installed firmware
    is in: it is the one recovery route that needs no working Bluetooth link
    and no charge in the device beyond what USB supplies.
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">Settings, over Bluetooth</h2>
  <h3>What a phone can change, and what it takes to make it stick.</h3>
  <p>
    Once flashed, a skyBlip Go offers one Bluetooth Low Energy service with
    two jobs. One streams live traffic as FLARM-format NMEA sentences, which
    is what SDVFR Next, SkyDemon, ForeFlight and similar apps already read.
    The other reads and writes the device&rsquo;s settings as small JSON
    messages: a phone can read the current configuration, or propose a new
    one.
  </p>
  <p>What a phone can change:</p>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Setting</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Address</th><td>self-minted, or an issued ICAO, FLARM or OGN address</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Aircraft category</th><td>the ADS-L type this device broadcasts as</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Alarm</th><td>on or off, and a volume from 0 to 5</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Stealth</th><td>broadcast anonymously, vertical rate withheld</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Units</th><td>metric or imperial, on the instrument pages</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Page mask</th><td>which instrument pages the screen cycles through</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Callsign</th><td>shown on the device and over the link &mdash; ADS-L itself carries no callsign field, so it is never transmitted on the air</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    None of it takes effect from a phone alone. Every change &mdash; a new
    alarm volume just as much as a firmware upload &mdash; is refused outright
    in the air. On the ground, it is staged as a prompt and waits for a
    gesture on the device itself: double-press the button within 600&nbsp;ms
    to confirm it, a single press refuses it, and a prompt nobody answers
    cancels itself after 30&nbsp;seconds. Losing the Bluetooth connection
    cancels it too. Bluetooth pairing is deliberately off on this device
    (encrypted settings break Web Bluetooth on some platforms), so this
    hands-on gesture is what stands in for it: nothing sensitive happens
    because a phone in range asked for it.
  </p>
  <p>
    A firmware upload goes through the same gesture twice: once to open a
    ten-minute window for the new image, once more to confirm the reboot that
    installs it. Taking off closes an open window immediately and cancels
    whatever prompt was waiting.
  </p>
  <p>
    The same button also changes page, walks the settings the device can edit
    on its own glass, and switches the device off.
    <%= link_to_page "buttons", class: "cta cta--next" %>
  </p>
  <p class="note">
    Reaching this today means a Bluetooth tool able to write to a custom
    GATT characteristic &mdash; the service reuses the same UUID block as
    Nordic&rsquo;s own UART service, so several already exist. A walk-up
    page at skyblip.eu that speaks this protocol for you is not built yet.
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">The alarm</h2>
  <h3>Three levels, and what each one actually does.</h3>
  <p>
    An alarm needs two things to fire at all: another aircraft transmitting
    something this device receives, and within 300&nbsp;m of your altitude.
    Above or below that band, traffic still shows on the radar screen, at
    whatever range &mdash; it is never alarmed.
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Level</th><th>Fires when</th><th>Buzzer</th><th>Vibration</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th>1 &middot; Info</th>
          <td>within 3&nbsp;km</td>
          <td>one short blip, once</td>
          <td>&mdash;</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>2 &middot; Important</th>
          <td>not moving away, and within 1.5&nbsp;km or less than 25&nbsp;s from closest approach</td>
          <td>two beeps of 250&nbsp;ms, a beep-length apart</td>
          <td>200&nbsp;ms, the moment it gets worse</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>3 &middot; Urgent</th>
          <td>actually closing, and within 500&nbsp;m or less than 15&nbsp;s from closest approach</td>
          <td>a train of six fast pulses, repeated every 2&nbsp;s while it stays urgent</td>
          <td>600&nbsp;ms, each time it escalates</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    &ldquo;Actually closing&rdquo; excludes GNSS noise: a target has to be
    shutting the gap at more than roughly 2&nbsp;m/s (about 4&nbsp;kt) before
    its closure counts for anything, and a target that stops closing for six
    seconds straight is held back at Info, however close it already got.
    &ldquo;Not moving away&rdquo; excludes traffic that is, whatever the
    range. And two aircraft turning the same way at the same rate, within
    1&nbsp;km and 100&nbsp;m of each other &mdash; a normal gaggle sharing a
    thermal &mdash; are held at Info too, by design: that geometry produces a
    dozen false urgents an hour if it doesn&rsquo;t.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each level is told apart by its rhythm, so a pilot learns three sounds
    and not three frequencies: the pitch does rise by 400&nbsp;Hz per level
    around the buzzer&rsquo;s 4&nbsp;kHz resonance, but the cadence is the cue
    that carries through a headset. Every one of them stops on its own: only
    Urgent repeats, and it leaves about a second of quiet between trains for a
    radio call.
  </p>
  <p class="note">
    This is straight-line geometry from each aircraft&rsquo;s current track
    and speed, re-evaluated every second. It has no model of where a circling
    aircraft will be in ten seconds, only of where it is now, and that has a
    consequence worth knowing before you fly it: two gliders working the same
    thermal on slightly offset circles can pass very close while the range
    between them never closes for long enough to count, and this device will
    hold them at Info throughout. In a gaggle, the screen is the instrument.
    The alarm is for the aircraft that is not turning with you.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="pledge">
  <h2 class="kicker">What this device does not see</h2>
  <h3>It sees only what already transmits something it can receive.</h3>
  <p>
    A skyBlip Go receives ADS-L directly, receives FLARM-generation traffic
    directly, and &mdash; only within range of a skyPost ground station
    &mdash; the ADS-B traffic that station relays over ADS-L. That is the
    whole list. It does not receive OGNTP, FANET or FANET+, PilotAware&rsquo;s
    P3I, 978&nbsp;UAT, or 1090&nbsp;MHz ADS-B on its own radio: an airliner or
    a touring aircraft squawking ADS-B is invisible to a Go outside skyPost
    coverage, exactly as a paraglider on FANET alone is invisible to it
    everywhere.
  </p>
  <p>
    And under every one of those protocols sits the case that matters most:
    an aircraft carrying only a transponder, or carrying nothing at all,
    transmits nothing any of this equipment can receive, and stays invisible
    to it, full stop. A clear alarm screen is not an empty sky. This device
    is <%= link_to_page "see-and-avoid", label: "see-and-avoid support", class: "cta" %>,
    the second pair of eyes described on that page, not a replacement for the
    lookout the rules already put on the pilot.
  </p>
  <p>
    <%= link_to_page "safety", class: "cta cta--next" %>
  </p>
</section>

